!----> Yeah, I like that one. Yeah, when I do that background, I want to do=
 it to where some person who loves pictures and lets the pictures speak to =
them having a wall or a piece of wall or a mirror in their bedroom and piec=
ing together all these different moments, all these different scenarios, th=
e late time having to be at the emergency room or holding some kind of cont=
ainment unit to let someone throw up, whether it be a male or female, wheth=
er it's a child or adult, dementia, cancer, you know, the going to a doctor=
's appointment that's five, six hours away, just the whole thing, man, like=
 when the point of no return and then you realize I'm not alone. Somebody c=
an help me if I reach out to them and walk me through this because I need t=
hat support of somebody who's been there, not somebody who's telling me how=
 it's going to be, but somebody who said, this is how it happened for me, t=
his is what I was able to do, and this is how I overcame it, and give you t=
hat extra, that strength just to make it one more day at least to give them=
, those you're caring for, a quality of life that they deserve, that they d=
o not have except through you because of the illness or disability or behav=
ioral. It gives you a chance to make it one more day to fulfill your respon=
sibility as a caregiver and give them the best quality of life possible. <!=
